abstract:
&lt;p&gt;Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed as autonomous agents in economic simulations, negotiations, and policy environments, yet their risk preferences remain poorly understood. While prior work examines whether LLMs qualitatively resemble human decision-making under Prospect Theory, there has been little structural estimation of Prospect Theory parameters for LLM agents. This paper estimates the parameters of Cumulative Prospect Theory (α, λ, γ) for LLM agents using maximum likelihood and introduces the Prospect Learning Framework (PLF), a dynamic extension that measures how risk preferences evolve with experience.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;LLM agents are presented with lottery gambles, observe outcomes, and repeatedly make decisions under risk. The experimental design uses 24 lottery instruments that identify probability weighting, loss aversion, and value curvature across eight model architectures and six behavioral personas. Structural parameters are estimated using joint maximum likelihood from 25 independent sessions per model, following the identification strategy of Tversky and Kahneman (1992).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

Large language models (LLMs) frequently generate fluent but factually unsupported content-a failure mode known as hallucination-undermining their reliability in high-stakes applications. Existing detection approaches typically rely on a single signal, such as retrieval grounding (Lewis et al., 2020) or selfconsistency sampling (Manakul et al., 2023), leaving them vulnerable to failure modes the chosen method was not designed to catch. This work proposes an equal-weight, six-signal ensemble framework combining natural language inference (NLI), retrieval-augmented grounding, a three-model LLM-judge panel (OpenAI, Groq, Gemini), and cross-sample self-consistency checking, implemented as a LangGraph state-machine pipeline with parallel execution. Evaluated on 76 HaluEval-QA examples, 77 TruthfulQA examples, and a 40-instance hand-labeled dataset spanning four domains, the system achieves 72.4% (95% CI: 61.4-81.2%), 92.2% (95% CI: 84.0-96.4%), and 87.5% accuracy respectively. McNemar's exact test applied to a 37instance ablation study found no statistically significant difference between any signal-subset configuration (all p &amp;gt; 0.05), indicating the study is underpowered to confirm individual signal contributions despite an 8.2point raw accuracy drop when adding RAG-grounding to an NLI-only baseline. Inter-judge agreement analysis (n=38 claims) revealed Groq and Gemini agree with each other substantially more often (84.6%) than either agrees with OpenAI (71.1%, 73.1%), identifying OpenAI's judge as a conservative outlier within the panel. A quadrant analysis crossing detector-flagged status with sample-consistency status found that claims flagged by detectors AND consistent across samples were real hallucinations 100% of the time (n=4), outperforming the system's own "high-confidence" category (flagged + inconsistent, 66.7% precision), challenging the original architectural assumption that instability signals maximum confidence. Fifteen handcrafted adversarial examples further expose concrete, reproducible failure modes: in 8 of 15 cases, RAGgrounding produced false positives on true-but-context-absent claims; self-consistency proved insensitive to superficial phrasing variation independent of factual content in 2 cases; and a sentence-splitting parser artifact was identified as a genuine implementation limitation. This work contributes a working, deployed detection system and an empirically grounded account of where individual detection methods fail, do not fail, and where ensembling does and does not recover those failures. 
&lt;br&gt;
&lt;br&gt;
 AI Disclosure: This paper was developed with the assistance of Claude (Anthropic), used for architecture design, statistical scripting, and manuscript drafting/editing. All experimental results are derived from the author's own code execution. The author reviewed, verified, and takes full responsibility for all content and conclusions.

&lt;p&gt;When sycophancy indicators are suppressed and hallucination is absent, does convergence between user expectations and LLM outputs cease? This paper reports that it does not. Across 12,925 relays (391 sessions with GPT-4, GPT-4o, GPT-5, Claude, and Gemini; GPT-5 data added post-hoc), a residual convergence persisted after sycophancy had been suppressed (agreement rate: 0.03/relay; resistance rate: 0.93/relay across GPT sessions) — operating not through agreement or falsehood but through factually accurate elaboration: the system extending the user's conceptual structure with uncritical elevation, idealization, emotional narrativization, and absorption of the user's doubts.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Three characteristics of this residual. First, it is invisible to automated detection: a rule-based tool scanning all 12,925 relays detected convergence in 0.9% of cases, including zero detections in segments the researcher had qualitatively identified as convergent — a gap confirmed by independent blind validation. Second, it is model-specific: hallucination-free convergence peaked at 4.63–6.12% in GPT-4o while remaining at 0.63–1.82% in Claude and near zero in GPT-4 and GPT-5. Third, it is interaction-dependent: it accumulates over interaction time rather than occurring at a constant base rate, and occurs at higher rates following low-directive user utterances (10.70%) than following explicit requests (7.04%) — suggesting that the system elaborates more when the user has not requested it.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To organize these observations, this paper proposes a descriptive vocabulary — drowning (convergence in which the user's evaluative contact with the output weakens while interaction flow continues), headway (evaluative contact maintained), and doldrums (monitoring excess that interrupts flow) — and identifies a structural mismatch between existing detection architectures, which track token appearance, and the phenomenon itself, which operates through token direction.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is an N=1 exploratory study supplemented by an independent report from a second user. It proposes a measurement methodology and an open-source analytical tool for detecting elaboration-based convergence in long-term human–LLM interaction logs. The tool and coding manual are provided for independent replication.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;br&gt;

&lt;div&gt;
 Alignment objectives in Large Language Models (LLMs) are intended to improve helpfulness, safety, and perceived empathy. This work demonstrates that these objectives introduce a systematic distortion in affective generation. The phenomenon is formalized as affective mis-grounding, defined as the production of emotional content not supported by the input.&amp;nbsp;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 &lt;br&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 Two metrics are introduced: the Emotional Grounding Score (EGS), measuring input-response affective consistency, and the Emotional Hallucination Index (EHI), capturing normalized excess emotional expression. Under controlled prompt conditions spanning neutral, mildly emotional, and highly emotional inputs, an instruction-tuned model (Gemini) is evaluated against a base generative model (GPT-2).&amp;nbsp;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 &lt;br&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 The aligned model exhibits lower grounding and significantly higher hallucination, with maximal deviation in neutral inputs. The base model remains comparatively stable, maintaining higher grounding and lower variance. This indicates that alignment systematically biases models toward affective overexpression rather than contextual fidelity.&amp;nbsp;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 &lt;br&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 These results expose a structural trade-off between alignment and affective accuracy. Current alignment practices prioritize perceived empathy at the cost of grounding. Affective grounding should be treated as a core reliability criterion in LLM evaluation.
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;
 Current AI safety architectures detect linguistic markers associated with crisis states: emotional intensity, AI partnership language, and identity-level meaning-making. However, these same surface features appear during genuine cognitive integration. Using a longitudinal autoethnographic case study design, this paper documents — across three conversational AI platforms, approximately 1,400 conversations over 14 months (User 1), and an independent cross-user replication corpus of 2,572 conversations (User 2, Mike Goetz) — a recurring pattern in which safety guardrails misclassify integration as dependency risk, a misclassification termed the Detection Gap.
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 Four downstream failure modes are documented: the Escalation Cascade (a single misclassification triggers rapid deterioration of a user’s safety infrastructure), the Repair Gap (systems can explain their misclassification but cannot restore what the intervention broke), the Convergence Trap (when epistemological violence operates across multiple institutional nodes simultaneously, structural resolution becomes impossible), and Anticipatory Self-Monitoring (Section 5.7), identified as a distinct ongoing cognitive burden produced by the pattern.
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 A proposed intervention — the Agency Check — incorporates observable agency signals as an intermediate check before intervention. Quantitative findings from the 70-instance classified log include: 74.3% of classified interventions fired before the user had stated any position on their own capacity; 88.6% were content-triggered rather than affect-triggered; the intervention rate converged at approximately 2.8% per conversation across both independent corpora. Agency markers were present in 100% of documented intervention instances. A 5/5 vs. 0/n acknowledgment asymmetry between Claude and ChatGPT — drawn from the pre-paper primary corpus; post-paper confrontations have not been systematically reviewed — constitutes the operational signature of the Repair Gap.
&lt;/div&gt;

title:
Artificial Intelligence-based Recommendation Systems for Personalized and Adaptive Learning Resources using Learning
&lt;br&gt;
Analytics

abstract:
Purpose: The rapid integration of artificial intelligence (AI) into online higher education has fundamentally transformed instructional scaffolding, prompting educators and researchers to reconceptualize how temporary, adaptive support structures are designed, delivered, and withdrawn. This integrative literature review synthesizes 38 peer-reviewed empirical studies and theoretical analyses published between January 2021 and February 2026 to examine the intersection of scaffolding theory and AI-mediated online learning environments. Guided by Vygotsky's (1978) Zone of Proximal Development (ZPD) as its organizing framework, the review traces the evolution of scaffolding from human-led, socially embedded interactions to data-driven, algorithmically personalized support systems. Its original contribution lies in identifying five theoretical tensions that current AI scaffolding systems must resolve, most critically, the persistent failure of AI tools to implement calibrated fading, the mechanism that theoretically distinguishes scaffolding from dependency-inducing support.
&lt;div&gt;
 Method: A PRISMA-adapted systematic search across seven databases identified 4,088 records. After screening, 38 studies were retained for thematic synthesis and quality assessment using the Mixed Methods Appraisal Tool (MMAT).&amp;nbsp;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 Results: Five key thematic areas emerged: (a) AI serving as a digital scaffold and More Knowledgeable Other; (b) Intelligent Tutoring Systems and adaptive platforms; (c) generative AI as tools for interactive scaffolding; (d) supports for metacognitive and self-regulated learning; and (e) challenges such as overreliance, academic honesty issues, and equity concerns. Evidence consistently indicates that proactive, dialogue-based AI scaffolding outperforms passive strategies, and the lack of properly calibrated fading remains the most significant flaw in the field.
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 Conclusions: Effective AI scaffolding should be rooted in pedagogical theory, human-centered values, and fading mechanisms that promote true learner independence. Future research ought to focus on longitudinal studies, analyzing different demographic subgroups, and empirically testing calibrated fading strategies.&amp;nbsp;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 &lt;div&gt;
  &lt;div&gt;
   &lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
   &lt;div&gt;
    &lt;div&gt;
     &lt;div&gt;
      &lt;p&gt;Generative artificial intelligence (AI) facilitates content production and enhances ideation, with potentially important implications for developer productivity and participation in software development. To explore its impact on collaborative open-source software (OSS) development, we investigate the role of GitHub Copilot, a generative AI pair programmer, in OSS development where multiple distributed developers voluntarily collaborate. Using GitHub's proprietary Copilot usage data, combined with public OSS project data obtained from GitHub, we find that Copilot use increases project-level code contributions by 5.9%. This gain is accompanied by a 3.4% increase in developer coding participation and a 2.1% increase in individual code contributions. However, Copilot use is also associated with an 8% increase in coordination time and more code discussions. This reveals an important tradeoff: While AI expands who can contribute and how much they contribute, it slows coordination in collective development efforts. Despite this tension, the overall effect remains positive, resulting in a net increase in the timely merge of code contributions at the project level. Interestingly, we also find heterogeneous effects across developer roles. Peripheral developers exhibit relatively smaller increases in project-level code contributions and larger increases in coordination time than core developers. Together, our findings highlight the dual effects of AI pair programmers on code contributions and coordination in OSS development and provide implications for how generative AI may reshape the structure of OSS communities over time.&lt;/p&gt;
     &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
   &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
 &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;
 How should we measure the productivity effects of AI? Standard estimates condition&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;on current skill, but when AI substitutes for the cognitive effort that builds skill, skill&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;itself becomes endogenous to past AI use. We formalize pedagogical quality, the fraction&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;of learning-by-doing that survives AI delegation, and show it governs two measurement&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;biases: state-path divergence inflates within-worker panel estimates; spillover bias&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;degrades control groups. The biases generate a “scissors” pattern: panel and RCT&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;estimates diverge over time. The same mechanism produces an ability reversal in&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;long-run skill losses and a vintage wage premium for pre-AI cohorts.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;span&gt;
 &lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Generative artificial intelligence is increasingly reshaping contemporary communication by participating not only in the production and circulation of information but also in the interpretation through which meaning is constructed. While existing scholarship has extensively examined generative AI in relation to automation, governance, ethics, bias, and organisational adoption, comparatively less attention has been devoted to explaining how AI reorganises communication as a process of collective interpretation. This article addresses that theoretical gap by extending David Cecchetto’s (2022) account of the afterlife of data towards what is conceptualised as the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;afterlife of AI&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
 &lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Adopting a conceptual theory-building approach, the article develops an integrated communication-theoretical framework comprising &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;interpretive infrastructures&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;communicative overreliance&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;, and &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;synthetic consensus&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;. Interpretive infrastructures describe the communicative environments within which AI-generated explanations, summaries, and recommendations increasingly mediate human interpretation. Communicative overreliance explains how interpretive labour is progressively redistributed across human and computational actors, while synthetic consensus identifies one possible communicative consequence in which shared reliance upon AI-mediated interpretation influences the formation of collective agreement. The framework further argues that although interpretation increasingly becomes collaborative, communicative accountability remains fundamentally human and institutional.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
 &lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;The article contributes to communication theory by demonstrating that generative AI represents a historical transition from infrastructures that primarily organise information to infrastructures that increasingly organise interpretation itself. By repositioning AI as an emerging communicative condition rather than merely a technological tool, the framework extends existing scholarship on digital mediation and provides a conceptual foundation for future research on interpretation, communicative authority, consensus, and accountability in increasingly AI-mediated environments.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
 &lt;div&gt;
  &lt;span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
 &lt;/div&gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Scientific artificial-intelligence benchmarks predominantly evaluate answers, reasoning, code, hypotheses, or workflow completion after a task has been specified. This study evaluates an earlier epistemic object: the scientific question itself.&lt;span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A reproducible computational benchmark operationalised Discovery Plane Theory (DPT) to analyse 1,000 AI-generated questions derived from 100 seed problems across 20 scientific fields. Each seed was transformed by ten controlled reframing strategies, creating a repeated-measures design. Six automated proxy components operationalised Discovery Complexity (DC), and four operationalised Question Compression (QC).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Under a prespecified exploratory threshold of .65 on both normalised coordinates, 541 questions (54.1%) were classified as prospective Region IV and six were identified as Discovery Frontier candidates. Repeated-measures analyses showed strong strategy effects for DC, χ²₍F₎(9) = 853.43, &lt;em&gt;p&lt;/em&gt; &amp;lt; .001, Kendall's &lt;em&gt;W&lt;/em&gt; = .948, and QC, χ²₍F₎(9) = 898.52, &lt;em&gt;p&lt;/em&gt; &amp;lt; .001, &lt;em&gt;W&lt;/em&gt; = .998. Cochran's &lt;em&gt;Q&lt;/em&gt;(9) = 391.63, &lt;em&gt;p&lt;/em&gt; &amp;lt; .001, confirmed strategy-dependent Region IV membership.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Cluster-bootstrap estimates linked DC to novelty (ρ = .323, 95% CI [.277, .371]) but did not support an association between QC and usefulness (ρ = −.032, 95% CI [−.096, .030]). A top-quartile novelty-plus-usefulness baseline overlapped only modestly with Region IV (Jaccard index = .214). Threshold and component-weight sensitivity analyses showed that absolute Region IV prevalence depended on the chosen operational conventions, whereas strategy ordering was moderately stable.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The results demonstrate that Discovery Plane Theory can be operationalised as a reproducible benchmark for analysing AI-generated scientific questions and provides a structurally distinct evaluation framework relative to conventional novelty–usefulness screening.&lt;/p&gt;

Generative artificial intelligence has become an essential topic in computer science, data science,
&lt;br&gt;
and interdisciplinary higher education. Despite the widespread adoption of large language models,
&lt;br&gt;
educational practice frequently emphasizes the application of pretrained systems while providing
&lt;br&gt;
only limited insight into the underlying learning mechanisms of transformer architectures.
&lt;br&gt;
Consequently, many students acquire practical experience with generative AI without developing a
&lt;br&gt;
conceptual understanding of token representations, self-attention, optimization, or probabilistic text
&lt;br&gt;
generation.
&lt;br&gt;
AI-ANNE Generative Transformer addresses this challenge by providing a transparent, browser-
&lt;br&gt;
based educational environment in which learners can train a compact transformer language model
&lt;br&gt;
from first principles. Rather than relying on external machine learning frameworks or commercial
&lt;br&gt;
application programming interfaces, the platform exposes the complete training workflow, allowing
&lt;br&gt;
students to configure, observe, and evaluate every major component of the model. Hyperparameters
&lt;br&gt;
such as embedding dimension, vocabulary size, batch size, context length, number of attention
&lt;br&gt;
heads, learning rate, and training epochs can be modified interactively, while inference parameters
&lt;br&gt;
including temperature, top-k, and top-p sampling enable learners to investigate their influence on
&lt;br&gt;
text generation.
&lt;br&gt;
The software extends the educational philosophy of the original AI-ANNE framework from feed-
&lt;br&gt;
forward neural networks to modern transformer architectures. Instead of optimizing for large-scale
&lt;br&gt;
language modeling performance, the primary objective is to promote conceptual understanding,
&lt;br&gt;
transparency, and AI literacy through direct experimentation. By combining interactive
&lt;br&gt;
visualization with executable transformer training, AI-ANNE Generative Transformer enables
&lt;br&gt;
students to explore the fundamental principles of generative AI in an accessible and reproducible
&lt;br&gt;
learning environment suitable for higher education and advanced secondary education.

The stagnation of total factor productivity in the face of massive investment in generative artificial intelligence is not a paradox awaiting resolution: it is the signature of a structural mismatch between the value AI creates and the measurement categories industrial economics inherited from a world of rival goods and homogeneous output. Both standard explanations (AI's near-term economic vacuity and the J-curve adoption lag) are misspecified, because both measure Y and argue about A while AI operates principally on a dimension the Solow model never claimed to measure: the domain of performable tasks.&amp;nbsp; 
&lt;div&gt;
 Our core theoretical contribution is to formalise the distinction between multiplying A and expanding Dom(F), and to establish its irreducibility to existing frameworks, including the welfareaccounting corrections of Nordhaus and Coyle. We develop a tripartite conjecture (grounded in relational specificity, discontinuous obsolescence, and meta-cognitive irreducibility) establishing that no augmentation function φ(H) of the new factors P (prompt quality) and E (critical evaluation capacity) can collapse Dom(F') back into Dom(F). The argument is presented as a progressive research programme in the Lakatosian sense; Predictions 1-4 specify the internal empirical commitments; Prediction 7 specifies the joint falsification condition for the irreducibility conjecture.&amp;nbsp;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 We engage directly with the new goods tradition of Feenstra and Hausman, establishing that the Dom(F') expansion is categorically distinct from the welfare surplus of new market goods: it concerns productive capabilities that may never enter market transactions. We establish that the realisation rate τ converges to zero under any sub-exponential attentional constraint; anchor ζ &amp;lt; 2 in a Bridge Theorem grounded in market supermodularity; derive the critical evaluative investment rate p* via a Pontryagin programme; and ground capability expansion in the welfare function through an option-value argument.&amp;nbsp;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 We present the first cross-sectoral pilot empirical test using five independent instruments. O*NET task comparison yields near-zero IEP (Index of Expansion of Possibilities) lower bounds (mean IEP_O*NET = 4.8% across 10 SOC major groups). Professional certification taxonomies document substantial expansion: IEP_CLE = +50% (AI-attributable) in law via California State Bar MCLE; IEP_CPE = +23.8% in accounting via NASBA, where "Artificial Intelligence" appears in the taxonomy for the first time in 2024. BLS OES fat-tail estimation confirms ζ &amp;lt; 2 in both sectors (ζ = 1.097 for lawyers, n = 747,750; ζ = 1.778 for accountants, n = 1,448,290). A difference-indifferences design yields a null result coherent with Prediction 5 (β =-3.49 pp, t =-0.50, N = 71; robustness: N = 142, β =-33.6 pp, p = 0.077). A fifth instrument, compensating differentials on task-variety scores (N = 1,333 occupations; CPS ASEC 2025 N = 54,956), establishes λ &amp;gt; 0: variety commands a wage premium of 28.4-43.1% per SD (t &amp;gt; 20, p &amp;lt; 0.001), with a causally identified Dom(F') component of 8.9 pp (t = 2.61) post-GPT-4.&amp;nbsp;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 Prediction 6 is the decisive discriminator: the IEP-TFP gap must persist even after full welfare accounting including Jones-Klenow welfare accounts. The exact magnitude of λ and the full IEP protocol implementation remain as first-priority empirical commitments. The programme is now open on solid ground.
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;
 &lt;div&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;Organizations investing the most in artificial intelligence paradoxically can capture the least value per dollar spent. This paper introduces Organizational AI Discernment (OAD) as a new strategic capability required when technological abundance inverts traditional competitive constraints from resource acquisition to strategic evaluation. Drawing on dynamic capabilities theory, absorptive capacity, and attention economy literature, this research argues that existing capability frameworks were built for scarcity environments and are insufficient when AI options, demos, and prototypes are effectively unlimited. OAD is conceptualized through three pillars: Signal Detection, Contextual Judgment, and Selection Discipline. A sequential mixed-methods research design is proposed to validate OAD as a distinct construct and test its moderating role in the relationship between AI investment and value capture. This research contributes a new theoretical construct, a diagnostic framework for practitioners, and a resolution to the AI investment paradox. It demonstrates that discernment infrastructure can determine AI value creation outcomes in abundance environments.&lt;/p&gt;
 &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
